N:mex
D:[mex] Drive mex (`mex-agent`), persistent project memory and code graphs for AI coding agents. One command scaffolds a living wiki, builds a deterministic code graph, and installs a project anchor file (CLAUDE.md, root AGENTS.md, .cursorrules, .windsurfrules, copilot-instructions.md, or .opencode/opencode.json) that your agent auto-loads as a standing rule document. Node.js >= 22.5, `npm install -g mex-agent` (MIT); this skill exposes an unambiguous `mex-agent` wrapper because TeX Live may own `mex`. Supports TypeScript, JavaScript, Python, and Rust.
G:mex mex-agent project-memory code-graphs drift-detection documentation agent-memory living-wiki tree-sitter sqlite cli project-anchor

U[7]:
  Set up a new project scaffold for an AI-assisted codebase (`mex-agent setup`)
  Build a symbol-grounded living wiki tied to exact source locations
  Get a project anchor file (AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, .cursorrules, ...) auto-generated so an agent picks up mex context with no manual wiring
  Keep architectural notes, decisions, and conventions synchronized with code
  Run drift detection (`mex-agent check`) to catch stale documentation
  Sync the wiki back to code after meaningful changes (`mex-agent sync`)
  Retrieve compact, task-scoped context instead of a full-repo grep (`mex-agent graph scope "<task>"`)

S[6]{n,step,details}:
  1,One-shot auto-install,bash .agent-skills/mex/scripts/install.sh /path/to/project — resolves or installs mex-agent plus its wrapper, ensures the default root AGENTS.md bridge, runs setup/graph/check without invoking TeX Live `mex`
  2,Populate the scaffold,every context/*.md and patterns/* file is an empty template until you paste install.sh's printed prompt into your coding agent session — that agent session writes project-specific content
  3,Edit ROUTER.md,map common agent tasks (e.g. "add a new API endpoint") to the relevant context/patterns files so agents load only what a task needs
  4,Keep it current,`mex-agent graph` rebuild after code changes; `mex-agent check` validates drift but 0.7.1 may refresh graph.db; `mex-agent sync` repairs drift with a targeted agent prompt
  5,Do not promise MCP,packages/mex-mcp is unpublished to npm and ships no `mex mcp` CLI subcommand — only document the build-from-source path
  6,Read-only environment check,bash .agent-skills/mex/scripts/mex.sh doctor [project_path] — inspects Node/mex/Git/.mex/ state without installing or modifying anything

R[6]:
  Use install.sh, not manual steps — it is idempotent (skips `mex-agent setup` when .mex/ already exists) and reports which anchor file mex chose
  ROUTER.md is the anchor — keep task-to-context mappings short so agents never load the entire wiki
  Check before sessions (`mex-agent check`), sync after (`mex-agent sync`); use a scratch copy for strict read-only audits because check may refresh graph.db
  Version the wiki — .mex/ is Markdown/JSONL; commit it to Git to track how memory evolved
  Never claim MCP is wired up for Claude Code, Cursor, jeo, gjc, jeopi, or OpenCode until packages/mex-mcp is actually published
  Telemetry is opt-out by default — mention DO_NOT_TRACK=1 / MEX_TELEMETRY=0 / `mex-agent config set telemetry off` if the user cares

E[4]{type,command}:
  Install skill,npx skills add https://github.com/akillness/jeo-skills --skill mex
  Auto-install + wiki,bash .agent-skills/mex/scripts/install.sh ~/code/my-project
  Detect drift,mex-agent check   (then: mex-agent sync)
  Doctor,bash .agent-skills/mex/scripts/mex.sh doctor /path/to/project
